## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to weave fetch, WebSocket, and Service Worker patterns into a beginner task, emphasizing offline resilience, id deduplication, and batched DOM updates in a realistic live feed scenario.

## Key Concepts
- fetch for initial payload and error handling
- WebSocket lifecycle with reconnect/backoff
- DOM diffing via batching and dedup by id
- Service Worker caches for offline access and offline fallback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal fetch wrapper
async function loadInitialAlerts(){ const r = await fetch('/api/alerts?limit=20'); return r.json(); }

class AlertsWS { constructor(url, onNew){ this.url=url; this.onNew=onNew; this.backoff=1000; this.connect(); }
  connect(){ this.ws=new WebSocket(this.url); this.ws.onmessage=e=> this.onNew(JSON.parse(e.data)); this.ws.onclose=()=>this.reconnect(); this.ws.onerror=()=>this.reconnect(); }
  reconnect(){ setTimeout(()=>{ this.backoff=Math.min(60000, this.backoff*2); this.connect(); }, this.backoff); }
}

function renderBatch(alerts, container){ const frag=document.createDocumentFragment(); alerts.forEach(a=>{ const d=document.createElement('div'); d.textContent=`${a.timestamp} - ${a.message}`; frag.appendChild(d); }); container.appendChild(frag); }
```

